  "designMd": {
    "raw": "# Arc’IT AI — Open landscape / R4\n\nStatus: composition proposal for owner review in OpenPencil. The existing\nReact site, localhost build, production and R3 designs remain unchanged.\n\n## Brief\n\nGive the accepted service landing page more presence, chiefly in the navigation,\nhero and actions. Keep the practical copy, three offers (two compact, one full\nwidth), original Cut Six mark, landscape scenes, player and motion language.\nThis is a composition study, not a new product or a rewrite of the offer.\n\n## One idea\n\nA bright editorial opening beside an unobscured landscape. The message has its\nown paper surface; the scene no longer needs a dark wash to carry body copy.\nAn oversized, exact white Cut Six mark gives the scene a graphic focal point.\nHierarchy comes from asymmetric proportions, scale and a decisive primary CTA.\n\n## Reference adaptation\n\n- Agentic Engineer, https://agentic-engineer.ai/, inspected on 25 September\n  2026 at desktop and mobile: left-aligned direct headline, a separate abstract\n  hero object, clear primary/secondary actions and restrained navigation.\n  Borrow the separation of message and graphic, not its ASCII globe, claims,\n  scarcity markers, customer proof or exact layout.\n- One, https://getone.one/ and /#pricing, inspected on the same date: immersive\n  scene, one clear central object, bold pill CTA and a compact detached navigation\n  surface on scroll. Borrow the strong visual anchor and tactile actions;\n  do not adopt translucent terminal panels, a mascot, its OS chrome or pricing.\n- Keep Arc’IT’s own assets and Geist family. No third-party media is copied.\n  Reference screenshots are local research evidence only.\n\n## Composition\n\n- Desktop: 1440px canvas, 80px outside margin, 1280px opening. Text-only\n  wordmark, an opaque compact charcoal navigation capsule (Offers / Process /\n  Scope / Start), and the Resources-style theme control. No glass treatment.\n- Headline: large Geist Sans, left aligned, same promise: “From prototype to\n  production-ready software.” The wordmark and selected section titles retain\n  Geist Pixel. This shifts emphasis without replacing the identity.\n- Hero: text and actions on paper, daylight scene in a tall rounded panel to\n  the right. The mark stays white, never green. No broad image tint. One small\n  solid caption surface holds the existing business/security line.\n- CTA: charcoal rounded control, inset sage arrow disk, clear focus outline in\n  implementation. The secondary action is a quiet text/link control; both keep\n  their existing destinations. Mobile keeps the primary action above the art.\n- Introduction: two-part composition, with existing explanation at left and\n  the current rounded 16:9 scene player at right. Hover/focus play behavior is\n  unchanged; the shown Play state is an interaction study, not a recorded VSL.\n- Offers: same two-plus-one arrangement, existing abstract motifs and copy.\n  Thin rules and larger headings give rhythm without a wall of cards. The full\n  factory section remains a quiet, distinct surface with the accepted pipe.\n- Inquiry and FAQs remain the current accepted R3.9 layout, including all four\n  answers initially open. Footer still shares the selected scene.\n\n## Color, type and motion\n\nPaper #f8f2e8, near-black #18211d, muted #615f56, green #236b59 and sage #b9d9c8.\nDark reverses the paper/text roles, not the logo rule; night imagery is not\ncovered by an extra green wash. Use natural daylight color as the largest accent.\nDesktop hero 76px Sans / 1.02, body 18–20px / 1.45; mobile hero 42px / 1.04,\nbody 17px. Do not shrink explanatory copy to fit a composition.\n\nRetain the actual logo assembly, offer studies and reversible factory flow.\nThe larger mark can use the existing small, slow displacement; no continuous\nspinning or mascot behavior. The landscape uses the received scene loop.\nReduced motion, data saving, visibility pause, theme/scene selection and real\nplayer controls keep their current behavior when/if implemented.\n\n## Selected companion and limitations\n\nOpenPencil contains an editable desktop composition through the factory offer,\na mobile composition through the offers, and a dark hero study. The unchanged\ninquiry/footer are not redrawn. A separate motion-state frame documents the\nexisting animation roles. Native boards are static: they do not prove playback,\nhover, responsive reflow or form submission. Working interactions remain in the\nunchanged localhost implementation.\n\nPreserve the R3.9 implementation until the owner accepts this direction. Review\nthe hero first, then the transition into the existing content. This is a proposed\nalternative, not the new operative production design.\n",
    "projectName": "Arc’IT AI — Open landscape / R4",
    "colorPalette": [
      {
        "name": "Paper",
        "hex": "#F8F2E8",
        "role": ", near-black #18211d, muted #615f56, green #236b59 and sage #b9d9c8."
      }
    ],
    "componentStyles": "Give the accepted service landing page more presence, chiefly in the navigation,\nhero and actions. Keep the practical copy, three offers (two compact, one full\nwidth), original Cut Six mark, landscape scenes, player and motion language.\nThis is a composition study, not a new product or a rewrite of the offer.\n\nA bright editorial opening beside an unobscured landscape. The message has its\nown paper surface; the scene no longer needs a dark wash to carry body copy.\nAn oversized, exact white Cut Six mark gives the scene a graphic focal point.\nHierarchy comes from asymmetric proportions, scale and a decisive primary CTA.\n\n- Agentic Engineer, https://agentic-engineer.ai/, inspected on 25 September\n  2026 at desktop and mobile: left-aligned direct headline, a separate abstract\n  hero object, clear primary/secondary actions and restrained navigation.\n  Borrow the separation of message and graphic, not its ASCII globe, claims,\n  scarcity markers, customer proof or exact layout.\n- One, https://getone.one/ and /#pricing, inspected on the same date: immersive\n  scene, one clear central object, bold pill CTA and a compact detached navigation\n  surface on scroll. Borrow the strong visual anchor and tactile actions;\n  do not adopt translucent terminal panels, a mascot, its OS chrome or pricing.\n- Keep Arc’IT’s own assets and Geist family. No third-party media is copied.\n  Reference screenshots are local research evidence only.\n\n- Desktop: 1440px canvas, 80px outside margin, 1280px opening. Text-only\n  wordmark, an opaque compact charcoal navigation capsule (Offers / Process /\n  Scope / Start), and the Resources-style theme control. No glass treatment.\n- Headline: large Geist Sans, left aligned, same promise: “From prototype to\n  production-ready software.” The wordmark and selected section titles retain\n  Geist Pixel. This shifts emphasis without replacing the identity.\n- Hero: text and actions on paper, daylight scene in a tall rounded panel to\n  the right. The mark stays white, never green. No broad image tint. One small\n  solid caption surface holds the existing business/security line.\n- CTA: charcoal rounded control, inset sage arrow disk, clear focus outline in\n  implementation. The secondary action is a quiet text/link control; both keep\n  their existing destinations. Mobile keeps the primary action above the art.\n- Introduction: two-part composition, with existing explanation at left and\n  the current rounded 16:9 scene player at right. Hover/focus play behavior is\n  unchanged; the shown Play state is an interaction study, not a recorded VSL.\n- Offers: same two-plus-one arrangement, existing abstract motifs and copy.\n  Thin rules and larger headings give rhythm without a wall of cards. The full\n  factory section remains a quiet, distinct surface with the accepted pipe.\n- Inquiry and FAQs remain the current accepted R3.9 layout, including all four\n  answers initially open. Footer still shares the selected scene.\n\nOpenPencil contains an editable desktop composition through the factory offer,\na mobile composition through the offers, and a dark hero study. The unchanged\ninquiry/footer are not redrawn. A separate motion-state frame documents the\nexisting animation roles. Native boards are static: they do not prove playback,\nhover, responsive reflow or form submission. Working interactions remain in the\nunchanged localhost implementation.\n\nPreserve the R3.9 implementation until the owner accepts this direction. Review\nthe hero first, then the transition into the existing content. This is a proposed\nalternative, not the new operative production design."
